<h1>is it better to be overqualified or underqualified for a first job</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">Neither. The better position is <em>exactly</em> qualified on the work, which almost never happens because job postings are fiction and your resume is theater. The real choice is between looking expensive and looking desperate, and both cost you. What actually wins is proof you can do the specific job, framed at the right altitude.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>53% of employers have dropped degree requirements, up from 30% the year before, but only 31% followed through with real hiring change, meaning "qualified" is increasingly self-defined and rarely verified. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla 2025</a></li>
    <li>Fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected real skills-based change even where requirements were dropped, employers still hire on signals they trust, not signals candidates think matter. <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Burning Glass Institute + HBS</a></li>
    <li>Resume use fell to 67% in 2025 as employers seek better signals, but the replacement is work samples and scored tasks, not more credentials. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla 2025</a></li>
    <li>An internship cuts underemployment odds by 48.5%, not because it adds credentials, but because it provides verifiable proof of doing the actual work. <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">Strada + Burning Glass, Talent Disrupted</a></li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>why "overqualified" is a trap, not a flex</h2>
  <p>Being overqualified for a first job sounds like a humblebrag. It is not. It is a credible threat that you will leave, demand more money than the role has room for, or make your manager look slow. Hiring is risk management, and an overqualified candidate reads as flight risk with a side of resentment.</p>
  
  <p>The economics are straightforward. A first job is priced for someone who needs training, runway, and patience. You show up with a master's degree, three side projects, and a published paper, and the employer sees someone who will be bored in six months, ask for a promotion in nine, and quit in twelve. They are not wrong. The data on early-career turnover is brutal, and managers get scored on retention.</p>
  
  <p>Worse, overqualification often masks a different problem: you do not actually know what the job requires. You have adjacent skills, not the specific ones. A physics PhD applying to data science looks overqualified on paper and underqualified in practice, because the job is not "be smart with numbers," it is "clean this pipeline, know this stack, navigate this political environment." The credential overshoots while the capability undershoots in ways that matter.</p>
  
  <p>The filter OS trains this mistake. School rewards accumulation: more degrees, more honors, more signals of general excellence. Work rewards precision: can you do this specific thing, for this specific team, with this specific constraint. The overqualified candidate is often just miscalibrated, carrying a heavy backpack into a sprint.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>why "underqualified" is worse than it looks</h2>
  <p>Being underqualified is not the same as being hungry or scrappy. Those are attitudes. Underqualified is a signal problem: you are asking someone to bet on potential when they have no mechanism to verify it. In a market where <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">new-grad hiring at big tech is down more than 50% from 2019</a>, that bet is increasingly rare.</p>
  
  <p>The underqualified candidate faces a specific trap: the Dunning-Kruger of job postings. Entry-level listings ask for two years of experience, proficiency in four tools, and a portfolio of shipped work. You read this and think "I am underqualified." But those requirements are aspirational fiction, written by people who do not do the job, screened by systems that pattern-match on keywords. The gap between the posting and the actual need is often enormous. Declaring yourself underqualified means you are taking a hallucinated standard seriously.</p>
  
  <p>More dangerously, underqualification becomes a self-narrative. You apply selectively, aim low, accept roles that teach you nothing, and stay underqualified by design. The first job is supposed to be a bridge from learning to doing. If you treat it as a charity case, you build a career on permission-seeking.</p>
  
  <p>The honest frame: most first-job candidates are underqualified by the posting's standard and overqualified by the actual work's standard, because the posting is nonsense and the work is learnable in weeks. The problem is not your position on the spectrum. It is that the spectrum itself is broken.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what employers actually screen for (it is not the job description)</h2>
  <p>Employers do not hire for qualifications. They hire for reduction of uncertainty. The resume, the degree, the interview, the reference check: each is a cheap attempt to answer "will this person work out" without paying them to find out. The entire system is a workaround for not being able to see you do the job first.</p>
  
  <p>This is why the <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">shift to skills-based hiring</a> is not about kindness or equity, though it has those effects. It is about better signal. Employers who dropped degree requirements and actually changed hiring behavior did so because they found better predictors: work samples, scored tasks, structured evaluations of actual capability. The 31% who followed through are the ones who replaced the old signal with something real, not just removed a barrier and hoped.</p>
  
  <p>For a first job, this changes everything. Your goal is not to be over or under some imaginary qualification line. It is to provide the specific signal that reduces uncertainty for this specific role. Sometimes that means showing you have done nearly identical work, even unpaid. Sometimes it means showing you can learn fast, with proof of having learned something hard recently. Sometimes it means demonstrating you understand the company's specific problem, not the generic industry.</p>
  
  <p>The degree-as-mortgage model sells the opposite: accumulate general credentials, trust they will convert. But conversion is breaking down. The <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market">New York Fed tracks recent-grad underemployment at 41.5%</a>, meaning two in five graduates work jobs that never required the degree. They were not underqualified. They were misfit, misaligned, carrying a filter credential into a market that wants proof.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>the real strategy: qualified on the work, not the posting</h2>
  <p>The winning position is not on the over-under axis at all. It is orthogonal: demonstrate capability at the exact altitude of the role, with evidence the employer can evaluate before hiring you.</p>
  
  <p>This means mapping the job's actual decisions, not its listed requirements. A marketing coordinator does not need "3+ years experience" and "proficiency in Salesforce." They need to write copy that converts, interpret a brief, manage a stakeholder who changes their mind. If you can show you have done those things, even in a student club or a personal project, you are qualified in the dimension that matters. If you cannot, ten years of adjacent experience does not fix it.</p>
  
  <p>The <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">48.5% underemployment reduction from internships</a> works because internships provide this exact signal: not a credential, but evidence of having operated in a real work environment, with real constraints, producing real output someone else used. The mechanism is proof, not prestige.</p>
  
  <p>For roles without internship pathways, the substitute is increasingly direct: scored work samples, simulation tasks, portfolio pieces with feedback history. The employer sees not that you completed a course, but that you made decisions, iterated, and produced something assessable. This is the structural replacement for the degree filter: not more filtering, but better evidence of the actual job.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how to position when you are genuinely behind</h2>
  <p>Sometimes you are underqualified in a real way: the role requires a technical skill you lack, an industry context you have not absorbed, a network you have not built. The wrong move is to apply anyway and hope your enthusiasm reads. The right move is to close the gap publicly, in a way the employer can see.</p>
  
  <p>This is where the old advice "take a lower title" misses the point. A lower title at the same company, or a "stepping stone" role in the same function, works only if it produces visible proof. If it just gives you a credential and no scored output, you have moved sideways on the same broken track. The <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Burning Glass finding that fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected real change</a> applies internally too: most lateral moves are theater.</p>
  
  <p>The honest path is to do the work before you have the job. Not all of it, not for free indefinitely, but enough to show the specific capability gap is closed. Build the model, write the analysis, run the small campaign, contribute to the open-source project. Then apply with that proof as the lead, not as a footnote. The framing shifts from "I am underqualified but eager" to "I have done this specific thing; here is the evidence."</p>
  
  <p>This requires rejecting the filter OS timing. School says wait, progress, get credentialed, then apply. The market rewards those who invert it: do, prove, then claim the role that matches the proof. The gap between those approaches is where the overqualified and underqualified labels lose their meaning entirely.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
  
  <h3>Should I take a job I am overqualified for to get my foot in the door?</h3>
  <p>Only if the job produces visible, scored work that qualifies you for the next role. A foot in the door with no mechanism to demonstrate capability is just a foot in a different closed room. If the role lets you build proof, the overqualification is temporary and strategic. If not, you are buying a credential with your time.</p>
  
  <h3>How do I know if I am actually underqualified or just intimidated by the posting?</h3>
  <p>Ignore the posting's requirements and find someone doing the job. Ask what decisions they make weekly, what output they produce, what "good" looks like. If you can do those things or have done close variants, you are qualified. If the gap is specific and narrow, close it with one project, not a degree.</p>
  
  <h3>Does being overqualified help in negotiation?</h3>
  <p>Sometimes, in mature companies with structured leveling. More often it hurts: you price yourself out of the band, or the hiring manager cannot get approval for your level. The better leverage is proof of specific value: "I built this, it drove this result, here is the evidence." That justifies any number. Credentials alone do not.</p>
  
  <h3>What if I have no way to show proof of work for my target role?</h3>
  <p>Create the context. Simulate the decision environment, do the analysis, publish the result. The bar is not "paid work for a recognized employer." It is "evidence a reasonable hiring manager can evaluate." A student who builds a pricing model for a fictional product and documents their reasoning has more signal than a graduate with a business degree and no artifacts.</p>
  
  <h3>Is it ever better to be underqualified and learn on the job?</h3>
  <p>Only if the employer has a genuine learning infrastructure and the role's cost of failure is low. Most first jobs meet neither condition. The "learn on the job" promise is often a way to pay less for someone who will struggle publicly. If you take this path, negotiate for mentorship access and a review of your work product at 90 days, not just a performance conversation.</p>
</section>

